    ],
    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Get into position safely: sit with the dumbbell on the right thigh, lie back and kick it up with the knee; at the end of the set bring it back to the thigh rather than dropping it (clinical opinion).",
        "Shoulder pain: shorten the range at the bottom and keep the upper arms out of the fully stretched position until it settles (clinical opinion).",
        "Keep the wrists straight over the forearms; bent-back wrists under a heavy dumbbell strain the wrist.",
        "Stop if sharp pain, numbness or tingling occurs.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Shoulder discomfort: use a neutral grip (palms facing each other) and tuck the elbows a little more.",
        "Lower back arching too much: put the feet on a low step or the end of the bench."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Two-arm incline dumbbell press (more stable).",
        "Incline push-up."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Heavier dumbbell.",
        "Slower lowering (3-4 s).",
        "Alternating incline press with two dumbbells."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "pec_r": "Main pusher, with a shift toward the upper (clavicular) fibres: a 30-degree incline gave the highest upper-pectoralis activity, while the middle and lower fibres fell as the bench was raised (Rodríguez-Ridao 2020, barbell); Lauver 2016 also recommends a 30-degree incline for the pectoralis major.",
      "ant_deltoid_r": "Works harder as the bench rises: at 30 degrees it was about as active as the upper chest, and it became the most active muscle at 45 and 60 degrees (Rodríguez-Ridao 2020).",
      "triceps_r": "Straightens the elbow; triceps activity did not change with bench angle (Rodríguez-Ridao 2020) and is lower with dumbbells than with a barbell (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "serratus_r": "Holds the shoulder blade against the rib cage while the arm presses.",
      "biceps_r": "Works as a stabiliser: biceps activity rose with the stability demand, highest with dumbbells (Saeterbakken 2011).",
      "forearm_flexors_r": "Grip the dumbbell handle.",
      "infraspinatus_r": "Rotator cuff: centres the head of the upper arm while the dumbbells balance freely (clinical opinion).",
      "rhomboids_midtrap_r": "Keep the shoulder blades drawn back and down on the bench.",
      "core": "Bracing keeps the ribs down and the trunk square while only one side is loaded.",
      "obliques_r": "Resist the trunk being pulled toward the loaded side; a one-arm press asks the trunk to resist rotation (clinical opinion).",
      "obliques_l": "Resist the trunk being pulled toward the loaded side (clinical opinion)."
    }
  },
